{
  "root": "main_stack",
  "state": {},
  "elements": {
    "main_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "lg",
        "padding": 16
      },
      "children": [
        "header_stack",
        "intro_text",
        "key_differences_section",
        "comparison_table_section",
        "critical_example_section",
        "sources_section"
      ]
    },
    "header_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "horizontal",
        "gap": "md",
        "align": "center"
      },
      "children": [
        "header_icon",
        "header_title"
      ]
    },
    "header_icon": {
      "type": "Icon",
      "props": {
        "name": "https://storage.googleapis.com/gen-ui-kit/assets/r_000005_01_1_laptop.svg"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "header_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "JavaScript Variable Declarations: `let`, `const`, & `var`",
        "variant": "h1"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "intro_text": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "In JavaScript, `var`, `let`, and `const` are used to declare variables, but they differ significantly in their scope, hoisting behavior, and rules around reassignment. `let` and `const`, introduced in ES6 (2015), provide more predictable, block-level scoping to address common issues found with the traditional `var` keyword. Understanding these differences is crucial for writing modern, bug-free JavaScript."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "key_differences_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "lg"
      },
      "children": [
        "key_differences_title",
        "var_section",
        "let_section",
        "const_section"
      ]
    },
    "key_differences_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Key Differences: Scope, Hoisting, and Reassignment",
        "variant": "h2"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "var_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "var_title",
        "var_scope",
        "var_hoisting",
        "var_reassignment"
      ]
    },
    "var_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "`var`",
        "variant": "h3"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "var_scope": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Scope**: Function-scoped. A variable declared with `var` is accessible anywhere within the function it's defined in, or globally if declared outside any function. It does not respect block scope (e.g., `if` statements, `for` loops)."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "var_hoisting": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Hoisting**: `var` declarations are \"hoisted\" to the top of their scope and initialized with a value of `undefined`. This means you can access the variable before it's declared without a runtime error, but its value will be `undefined`."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "var_reassignment": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Reassignment & Redeclaration**: You can reassign a new value to a `var` variable and also redeclare the same variable in the same scope without an error."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "let_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "let_title",
        "let_scope",
        "let_hoisting",
        "let_reassignment"
      ]
    },
    "let_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "`let`",
        "variant": "h3"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "let_scope": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Scope**: Block-scoped. A variable declared with `let` is only accessible within the block (`{...}`) in which it is defined."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "let_hoisting": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Hoisting**: `let` declarations are hoisted but not initialized. Accessing a `let` variable before its declaration results in a `ReferenceError`. This period between the start of the block and the declaration is called the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "let_reassignment": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Reassignment & Redeclaration**: You can reassign a new value, but you cannot redeclare the same variable in the same scope."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "const_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "const_title",
        "const_scope",
        "const_hoisting",
        "const_reassignment",
        "const_note"
      ]
    },
    "const_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "`const`",
        "variant": "h3"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "const_scope": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Scope**: Block-scoped, identical to `let`."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "const_hoisting": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Hoisting**: Behaves the same as `let`; it's hoisted but inaccessible in the Temporal Dead Zone."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "const_reassignment": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Reassignment & Redeclaration**: Cannot be reassigned or redeclared. It must be initialized at the time of declaration."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "const_note": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "**Important Note**: For objects and arrays declared with `const`, the variable itself cannot be reassigned to a new object or array, but the properties or elements *within* the object or array can be modified."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "comparison_table_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "md"
      },
      "children": [
        "comparison_table_title",
        "comparison_table"
      ]
    },
    "comparison_table_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Feature Comparison Table",
        "variant": "h2"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "comparison_table": {
      "type": "Table",
      "props": {
        "columns": [
          {
            "key": "keyword",
            "label": "Keyword"
          },
          {
            "key": "scope",
            "label": "Scope"
          },
          {
            "key": "hoisting",
            "label": "Hoisting Behavior"
          },
          {
            "key": "reassigned",
            "label": "Can Be Reassigned?"
          },
          {
            "key": "redeclare",
            "label": "Can Be Redeclared?"
          }
        ],
        "rows": [
          {
            "keyword": "`var`",
            "scope": "Function",
            "hoisting": "Hoisted & initialized as `undefined`",
            "reassigned": "Yes",
            "redeclare": "Yes"
          },
          {
            "keyword": "`let`",
            "scope": "Block",
            "hoisting": "Hoisted (Temporal Dead Zone)",
            "reassigned": "Yes",
            "redeclare": "No"
          },
          {
            "keyword": "`const`",
            "scope": "Block",
            "hoisting": "Hoisted (Temporal Dead Zone)",
            "reassigned": "No",
            "redeclare": "No"
          }
        ],
        "domain": "generic",
        "preferredPresentation": "table",
        "sourceFormat": "markdown",
        "sourceText": "| Keyword | Scope | Hoisting Behavior | Can Be Reassigned? | Can Be Redeclared? |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `var` | Function | Hoisted & initialized as `undefined` | Yes | Yes |\n| `let` | Block | Hoisted (Temporal Dead Zone) | Yes | No |\n| `const` | Block | Hoisted (Temporal Dead Zone) | No | No |"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "critical_example_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "lg"
      },
      "children": [
        "critical_example_title",
        "critical_example_intro",
        "var_example_section",
        "let_example_section"
      ]
    },
    "critical_example_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Critical Example: Block Scope in Loops",
        "variant": "h2"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "critical_example_intro": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "This code demonstrates a common problem with `var` in loops with asynchronous actions like `setTimeout`. Using `let` solves this issue because it creates a new variable for each loop iteration."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "var_example_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "var_example_title",
        "var_example_text",
        "var_example_code_card"
      ]
    },
    "var_example_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "With `var`: Unexpected Behavior",
        "variant": "h3"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "var_example_text": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "The output will be \"3\" three times because the `i` variable is function-scoped. By the time the `setTimeout` callbacks execute, the loop has already finished, and `i` has a final value of 3."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "var_example_code_card": {
      "type": "Card",
      "props": {},
      "children": [
        "var_example_code"
      ]
    },
    "var_example_code": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    console.log(i); // Logs 3, 3, 3\n  }, 100);\n}"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "let_example_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "let_example_title",
        "let_example_text",
        "let_example_code_card"
      ]
    },
    "let_example_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "With `let`: Expected Behavior",
        "variant": "h3"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "let_example_text": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Using `let` creates a new `i` for each iteration of the loop, \"closing over\" its value for the `setTimeout` callback. This results in the expected output."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "let_example_code_card": {
      "type": "Card",
      "props": {},
      "children": [
        "let_example_code"
      ]
    },
    "let_example_code": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    console.log(i); // Logs 0, 1, 2\n  }, 100);\n}"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "sources_section": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "md"
      },
      "children": [
        "sources_title",
        "sources_text"
      ]
    },
    "sources_title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Conclusion & Best Practices",
        "variant": "h2"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "sources_text": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Modern JavaScript development strongly favors `let` and `const` over `var`. Use `const` by default for all variable declarations. Only use `let` if you know the variable's value will need to change. Avoid using `var` entirely in new code to prevent scope and hoisting-related bugs."
      },
      "children": []
    }
  }
}
